Injection Attacks
Injection attacks occur when untrusted input is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query, allowing the attacker to change the intended behavior of the application. 
In simple terms:

User input becomes part of a command
Interpreter executes the command
If input is malicious → attacker controls execution
These interpreters can include:

SQL databases
Operating system shells
LDAP queries
XPath queries
Template engines
Browsers (JavaScript)
Because the application combines user data with commands, attackers can manipulate the command structure itself. 

Core Concept of Injection
Injection occurs when an application does this:

Command + User Input = Executed Query
Example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '<user_input>'
If user input is:

admin' OR '1'='1
The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' OR '1'='1'
Now the query logic changes.
The database may return all records instead of one. 

Why Injection Happens
Injection vulnerabilities exist because the application:

1. Accepts untrusted input
2. Inserts it into a command
3. Executes the command without validation
Common developer mistakes:

Dynamic query construction
String concatenation
Lack of parameterization
Missing input validation
Unsafe APIs

Generic Injection Attack Flow

User Input
     │
     ▼
Application builds command
     │
     ▼
Interpreter executes command
     │
     ▼
Malicious input becomes executable instruction
     │
     ▼
Attacker controls backend system

Types of Injection Attacks
Injection can occur in many systems.
Common types include:

SQL Injection
Command Injection
NoSQL Injection
LDAP Injection
XPath Injection
Server-Side Template Injection
Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
Even XSS is technically an injection attack, because it injects JavaScript into the browser interpreter. 

SQL Injection (SQLi)
SQL Injection occurs when user input is inserted directly into SQL queries.
Example vulnerable code:

String query =
"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username='" + username +
"' AND password='" + password + "'";
If attacker inputs:

' OR 1=1 --
The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE username='' OR 1=1 --'
AND password=''
The condition 1=1 is always true.
This bypasses authentication.
SQL injection can allow attackers to:

Read sensitive data
Modify records
Delete data
Execute database admin commands
This is why SQL injection is considered one of the most severe web vulnerabilities. 

SQL Injection Example
Normal login query:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE username='alice'
AND password='mypassword'
Attacker input:

username: ' OR 1=1 --
password: anything
Query becomes:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE username='' OR 1=1 --'
AND password='anything'
Result:

Authentication bypass

SQL Injection Categories
SQL injection attacks can be classified into:
1. In-Band SQL Injection
Data is extracted using the same communication channel.
Example:

Error-based SQLi
Union-based SQLi

2. Blind SQL Injection
Application does not display query results.
Attacker infers data using:

Boolean responses
Timing delays
Example:

' AND 1=1 --
' AND 1=2 --

3. Out-of-Band SQL Injection
Data is exfiltrated through another channel.
Example:

DNS requests
HTTP callbacks

Command Injection
Command injection occurs when user input is passed to the operating system.
Example vulnerable code:

os.system("ping " + user_input)
Attacker input:

8.8.8.8; cat /etc/passwd
Executed command:

ping 8.8.8.8
cat /etc/passwd
The attacker now executes arbitrary commands.

Command Injection Example
Normal request:

GET /ping?host=example.com
Server executes:

ping example.com
Attacker request:

GET /ping?host=example.com;whoami
Executed:

ping example.com
whoami
Now the attacker can run system commands.

NoSQL Injection
Modern applications using MongoDB, CouchDB, DynamoDB can also be vulnerable.
Example:

db.users.find({ username: input })
Attacker input:

{ "$ne": null }
Query becomes:

db.users.find({ username: { "$ne": null } })
This returns all users.

Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI)
Template engines process dynamic templates.
Example:

Hello {{username}}
If user input is not sanitized:

{{7*7}}
The template engine evaluates it:

49
Advanced payloads can lead to Remote Code Execution.

LDAP Injection
Applications that use LDAP authentication may be vulnerable.
Example:

(&(uid=" + user + ")(password=" + pass + "))
Attacker input:

*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*
LDAP query logic is altered.
This may allow authentication bypass.

XPath Injection
XPath queries are used in XML databases.
Example:

/users/user[username='INPUT']
Attacker input:

' or '1'='1
Query becomes:

/users/user[username='' or '1'='1']
All records may be returned.

Injection Attack Mental Model
Think of injection like this:

Interpreter expects data
Attacker sends commands disguised as data
Interpreter executes commands
Diagram:

Attacker Input
     │
     ▼
Application Concatenates Input
     │
     ▼
Interpreter Executes Query
     │
     ▼
Attacker Gains Control

Secure Coding Patterns + Interview Answer Strategy

Injection vulnerabilities happen when untrusted input is interpreted as code or commands by an interpreter.

Typical interpreters include:

• SQL engines
• Operating system shells
• LDAP parsers
• XML/XPath processors
• template engines

The secure coding goal is simple but extremely important:

User input must never be interpreted as executable syntax.

Instead it must be treated strictly as data.

1. Secure Coding Pattern: Parameterized Queries (SQL Injection)
Why this works

SQL injection happens when the database parser cannot distinguish between code and data.

Example of vulnerable code:

String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + userInput + "'";

If user input is:

test@example.com' OR '1'='1

The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE email='test@example.com' OR '1'='1'

Now the database executes attacker-controlled logic.

Secure Pattern

Use prepared statements (parameterized queries).

The SQL engine parses the query structure first, then binds parameters.

Java Secure Example
String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?";
PreparedStatement stmt = connection.prepareStatement(query);
stmt.setString(1, userInput);
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery();

Why this is secure:

Query is compiled first

Input is bound separately

Input is treated as data only

SQL syntax cannot be modified

Even if attacker inputs:

' OR '1'='1

Database treats it as string value, not SQL code.

Python Secure Example
query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s"
cursor.execute(query, (user_input,))

Here the database driver escapes and binds parameters safely.

Additional Hardening

Even when using parameterized queries:

• Use least privilege database accounts
• Disable multiple statements
• Avoid dynamic SQL generation

Example of dangerous pattern even with prepared statements:

SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY " + userInput

ORDER BY cannot use parameters in some drivers → must whitelist allowed values.

2. Secure Coding Pattern: Command Injection

Command injection happens when applications pass user input into shell commands.

Example vulnerable code:

os.system("ping " + user_input)

If attacker inputs:

8.8.8.8; rm -rf /

Command becomes:

ping 8.8.8.8; rm -rf /

Shell executes both commands.

Secure Pattern 1: Avoid Shell Execution

Use native system libraries instead of shell commands.

Example:

Instead of:

system("mkdir " + folder)

Use:

Files.createDirectory(path)
Secure Pattern 2: Use Safe Process APIs

Example in Python:

subprocess.run(["ping", user_input])

Why this works:

Arguments are passed as array parameters, not interpreted by shell.

Shell metacharacters like ; or && are treated as plain text.

Secure Pattern 3: Input Allowlisting

If command requires limited options:

Example:

allowed = ["google.com", "yahoo.com"]
if user_input in allowed:
    ping(user_input)
3. Secure Coding Pattern: LDAP Injection

LDAP queries are structured like:

(&(uid=user)(password=pass))

Vulnerable code:

filter = "(uid=" + username + ")"

Attacker input:

*)(uid=*

Query becomes:

(uid=*)(uid=*)

Which matches all users.

Secure Pattern

Escape LDAP special characters.

Example in Java:

String safeInput = LdapEncoder.filterEncode(userInput);

Or use frameworks with built-in query builders.

4. Secure Coding Pattern: XPath Injection

XPath queries often appear in XML authentication systems.

Vulnerable code:

"//user[username='" + input + "' and password='" + pass + "']"

Attacker input:

' or '1'='1

XPath becomes:

user[username='' or '1'='1']

Authentication bypass.

Secure Pattern

Use parameterized XPath expressions or XML libraries that separate query structure from input.

Example:

XPathExpression expr = xpath.compile("//user[username=$u]");

Bind variables instead of string concatenation.

5. Secure Coding Pattern: Template Injection

Example vulnerable code in template engines:

Hello {{ user_input }}

If template engine evaluates expressions:

{{7*7}}

It executes.

In severe cases attackers run OS commands.

Secure Pattern

Use logic-less templates.

Examples:

• Mustache
• Handlebars

These engines do not allow arbitrary code execution.

Additionally:

Disable dangerous template functions.

6. Secure Architecture Pattern

At architecture level, injection risks must be handled in multiple layers.

Layer 1 – Input Validation

• Allowlist expected formats
• Reject special characters when unnecessary

Example:

Email validation.

Layer 2 – Safe APIs

Developers must never construct queries manually.

Use frameworks:

• ORM (Hibernate, SQLAlchemy)
• prepared statements

Layer 3 – Privilege Separation

Database accounts should not have:

• DROP TABLE
• ALTER
• ADMIN

Use read-only accounts where possible.

Layer 4 – Monitoring

Detect injection attempts via:

• Web Application Firewalls
• anomaly detection
• query monitoring

7. Production Security Checklist (Important for Interviews)

A production-ready system should ensure:

All database access uses parameterized queries

No shell commands use raw user input

Dangerous template engines are restricted

Input validation implemented at API boundary

Database accounts follow least privilege

Logging and monitoring detect suspicious queries

Static code scanning detects injection patterns

WAF rules detect common payloads

This checklist shows architectural thinking, which interviewers like.

8. How to Structure Injection Answers in Interviews

A strong interview answer must follow logical reasoning, not just definitions.

Example structure:

Step 1 – Define the problem

Injection occurs when untrusted input is interpreted as executable syntax by an interpreter.

Step 2 – Explain why it happens

It occurs when applications concatenate user input into commands or queries without separating code from data.

Step 3 – Explain exploitation

Attackers inject special characters or syntax to modify query logic.

Example:

' OR 1=1

Which changes authentication query logic.

Step 4 – Explain secure coding

Use parameterized queries, safe APIs, escaping, and allowlists.

Step 5 – Explain architectural controls

Add:

• least privilege databases
• monitoring
• WAF protection

Step 6 – Mention real-world examples

Example:

• login bypass
• database exfiltration
• OS command execution

This structured explanation demonstrates:

• security fundamentals
• secure coding knowledge
• architectural thinking

9. Common Follow-up Questions Interviewers Ask

These questions come up very often in security architect interviews.

Question 1

Why is input validation alone not enough?

Answer:

Because validation cannot predict every malicious input. Even valid inputs may contain dangerous characters. The correct approach is separating code from data using parameterized queries.

Question 2

Is ORM immune to SQL injection?

Answer:

No.

ORM frameworks still allow raw queries or dynamic query building.

Example:

session.createQuery("FROM User WHERE name='" + input + "'")

Still vulnerable.

Question 3

Can WAF stop injection attacks?

Answer:

WAF helps detect common payloads but should never be the primary defense. Secure coding practices must eliminate injection vulnerabilities at the application level.

Question 4

Why does least privilege help mitigate injection?

Answer:

Even if an attacker injects SQL commands, restricted database privileges prevent destructive operations like dropping tables or modifying schema.

Key Takeaway

Injection vulnerabilities are fundamentally caused by mixing user data with executable code.

Secure design requires:

separating code from data

using safe APIs

validating inputs

applying least privilege

monitoring suspicious behavior


